(106549, 4580, 3416, '<td valign="top">Perphaps the best picture of the challenge -my opinion, of course-. I can''t give it 10 because there is a small zone overexposed where is impossible to see anything. (9).jcof</td>', 'Perphaps the best picture of the challenge -my opinion, of course-. I can''t give it 10 because there is a small zone overexposed where is impossible to see anything. (9).jcof', '2002-08-22 07:35:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106550, 4580, 801, '<td valign="top">I like very much things in this picture (deep of field, composition, B&amp;W used...). You have got a excellent ''pencilist'' ambient (particularly because there is nothing distracting). I give it 9 because the overexposed left down corner. manolo</td>', 'I like very much things in this picture (deep of field, composition, B&W used...). You have got a excellent ''pencilist'' ambient (particularly because there is nothing distracting). I give it 9 because the overexposed left down corner. manolo', '2002-08-22 08:56:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106551, 4580, 3448, '<td valign="top">Nice, but why is it washed out at the bottom?</td>', 'Nice, but why is it washed out at the bottom?', '2002-08-22 15:20:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106552, 4580, 3080, '<td valign="top">This is a really neat idea and I realize you were also trying to get the "light" into the photo too, but the washed out part on the book is a bit too light for my taste.  This definately works with black and white.  Aelith did a very similar angle of a book in the transparency challenge (only with glasses instead of a pencil).  She got both compliments and criticism on having the book upside down.  Personally, I like it because she made the good point, that if the book were right side up, our focus would have been drawn to the words, instead of the subject.  The soft focus works well in the background like that.  Good job and good luck in the challenge.</td>', 'This is a really neat idea and I realize you were also trying to get the "light" into the photo too, but the washed out part on the book is a bit too light for my taste.  This definately works with black and white.  Aelith did a very similar angle of a book in the transparency challenge (only with glasses instead of a pencil).  She got both compliments and criticism on having the book upside down.  Personally, I like it because she made the good point, that if the book were right side up, our focus would have been drawn to the words, instead of the subject.  The soft focus works well in the background like that.  Good job and good luck in the challenge.', '2002-08-22 15:57:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106553, 4580, 2798, '<td valign="top">Nice work! Very brave to submit this here when voters typically dont like shallow DoF and overblown highlights. This seems intentional, though and I like it. 9 from me.</td>', 'Nice work! Very brave to submit this here when voters typically dont like shallow DoF and overblown highlights. This seems intentional, though and I like it. 9 from me.', '2002-08-22 17:04:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106554, 4580, 3190, '<td valign="top">nice black and white</td>', 'nice black and white', '2002-08-22 22:38:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106555, 4580, 259, '<td valign="top">a great photo.  if only the book wasn''t so washed out in the front</td>', 'a great photo.  if only the book wasn''t so washed out in the front', '2002-08-23 03:54:00.000000', NULL, 1),
(106556, 4580, 3087, '<td valign="top">Just lovely light and shadows, creating great shapes. Too much white out at the front of the book for me though.<br/>6, Kavey</td>', 'Just lovely light and shadows, creating great shapes. Too much white out at the front of the book for me though.6, Kavey', '2002-08-23 07:39:00.000000', NULL, 1),
